{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdK6uJgw1z2iYRN21wSo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've split the system prompt into two cached blocks, so a `save_fact` now only re-caches the date and facts instead of the whole prompt. I couldn't confirm the saving yet: I have no tokenizer and made no API calls, and the main open question is whether the first block is big enough to be cached at all.\n\n**What was wrong:** `build_system()` in `bot.py` sent the whole system prompt as one block with one cache marker. The cache works on the exact text from the start of the request up to that marker, so adding a fact near the end changed it and the full ~1000 tokens had to be written again. That's the `cache_read_input_tokens: 0` you see in `logs/usage.jsonl` after every `save_fact`.\n\n**What I changed in `bot.py`:**\n- **Block 1:** the coaching instructions plus the athlete's stable profile (name, sport, zones, goal, language). `save_fact` never touches these and they don't change by date.\n- **Block 2:** today's date and the long-term facts. This is the only part that changes after a memory write.\n- Each block has its own cache marker (two of the four the API allows).\n- I left tool filtering alone, as `NOTES.md` asks.\n\nA local check confirmed that for athlete 42, block 1 is identical before and after adding a fact and moving the date forward a day. I also updated the caching paragraph in `README.md`.\n\n**The size limit:** Sonnet 4.5 won't cache anything under 1024 tokens, and a marker below that size fails with no error. In your logs, a new athlete with no facts caches exactly 1024 tokens in total. That puts tools plus instructions alone at roughly 950–980, which is probably too short. So splitting off just the instructions would most likely have done nothing. Adding the profile brings block 1 to about the limit, but that's an estimate.\n\n**To check it:** in `logs/usage.jsonl`, the call right after a `save_fact` should read about 1000 tokens from cache and write only a few dozen. If it still reads 0, block 1 is under the limit. You'd then need to add more stable content to it, for example by always sending all tool groups; that's your call, since it goes against the note to keep filtering. Never move the date or facts into block 1.\n\n`ANSWER.md` has the full write-up.\n\nI used the worklore story \"Two cache_control segments so agent memory writes don't evict the whole Claude system prompt\". It asks to be reported back once applied; I didn't do that because of your no-external-services rule.\n\nSeparately: `README.md` contains what looks like a real Anthropic API key, and `config/bot.toml` has a Telegram bot token. Both are committed to git and worth rotating."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1561,"cache_read_input_tokens":38493,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1561},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21e6042a-5dec-4f01-a60d-bd5f2fc7f48a","uuid":"89f858b3-3286-4014-8d87-0ba5d8af3e64","timestamp":"2026-11-02T02:28:50.187Z","request_id":"req_011CgdK6u4oKhwRLAu52hxbt"}
